// PARAM: --analysis int
// Adapted from Goblint tests/regression/20-slr_term/03-3ctxs.c (a NOCHECK
// termination test; the post-loop check i == 10 is added). The same
// program under --context entry-state (16-composite-domain/precision/
// 03-3ctxs_parity_bounds_entry_state.vimp) proves it, and inlining f
// proves it in the context-insensitive analysis too, so the composite
// itself has the precision.
//
// Mechanism: f has one context-insensitive FunctionEntry unknown, fed by
// side effect from the call site inside the loop. The head's widening
// makes the call site deliver i = [0,9] on its second visit, the entry
// unknown widens [0,0] against that to [0,+inf], and side-effected
// unknowns only ever grow -- narrowing at the loop head later cuts the
// call site back to i = [0,8], but f's entry keeps j = [0,+inf] (measured
// with --dot-full: call site i = [0,8], entry_f j = [0,+inf]). f then
// returns [1,+inf], the back-edge carries [2,+inf], and the head cannot
// narrow below +inf, leaving i = [10,+inf] with parity Even after the
// loop: parity alone cannot single out 10. Distinct from 03-procedures/
// known-imprecision/01-two_call_sites_same_procedure.vimp, which merges
// two call sites; here a single call site loses to widening on the entry
// unknown that the caller's own narrowing never reaches.
void f(j) {
  return j + 1
}
void main() {
  int32 i, t;
  i := 0;
  while (i < 10) {
    t := f(i);
    i := 1 + t
  };
  __voblint_check(i == 10) // UNKNOWN
}
